Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern that helps decouple components by providing their dependencies from the outside rather than having them construct or find those dependencies themselves.

Core idea
- A "dependency" is any object a class needs to do its work (e.g., a repository, service, logger).
- Instead of a class creating or locating its dependencies, the dependencies are "injected" into the class (passed in), typically via the constructor, a setter method, or an interface.
- This is an application of Inversion of Control (IoC): the class relinquishes control over how dependencies are obtained.

Why use DI
- Testability: you can pass in mock or fake dependencies in unit tests.
- Modularity: components depend on abstractions (interfaces) rather than concrete implementations.
- Flexibility: swapping implementations (e.g., different data sources) is easy.
- Single responsibility: classes focus on behavior, not wiring.

Simple example (without DI)
- class Car creates its own Engine: Car() { engine = new GasEngine(); }
  - Hard to replace Engine for tests or alternate implementations.

With DI (constructor injection)
- class Car { Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; } }
  - Engine is provided from outside (caller, factory, or DI container).

Types of injection
- Constructor injection: dependencies passed through the constructor (preferred for mandatory dependencies).
- Setter (or property) injection: dependencies set via setter methods (optional or changeable dependencies).
- Interface injection: the dependency provides an injector method defined by an interface (rare in practice).

DI containers / frameworks
- Frameworks (Spring, Guice, Dagger, .NET Core DI) can automatically construct object graphs and inject dependencies based on configuration or annotations.
- Containers are useful for large apps, but introduce runtime configuration and a learning curve.

DI vs Service Locator
- Service Locator pattern pulls dependencies from a central registry inside the class (the class asks for them).
- DI favors having dependencies provided to the class, which keeps classes unaware of the container and easier to test.

Relation to SOLID
- DI supports the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): depend on abstractions, not concretions.

Drawbacks / cautions
- Overuse or excessive abstraction can make code harder to understand.
- DI containers add complexity and can hide where objects come from.
- Improper configuration can cause runtime errors.

Best practices
- Prefer constructor injection for required dependencies.
- Use interfaces (or abstract types) to depend on abstractions.
- Keep wiring code separate from business logic (composition root).
- Inject small, focused dependencies—avoid large "god" services.

